Both top and htop are used to monitor running processes and system performance in real time.

Think of them as a live dashboard of your Linux server.

1. top command

Run:

top

You will see something like:

PID   USER    %CPU   %MEM   COMMAND
1234  bilal   80.0    5.2   python
2345  root    20.0    2.1   nginx
3456  bilal    5.0    1.0   bash

Important columns

PID → Process ID

Every running process gets a unique number.

USER → User who started the process

%CPU → How much CPU the process is using

%MEM → How much RAM the process is using

=> What can you see in top?

At the top, you can see information about:

CPU
Memory
Swap
Load Average
Running processes

For example:

%Cpu(s): 20% us, 5% sy, 75% id

Here:

us → CPU used by user processes
sy → CPU used by system/kernel
id → CPU idle/free

2. htop command

htop does basically the same job as top, but it is more user-friendly.

Run:

htop

It provides a colorful interactive display with:

CPU  ███████
MEM  █████
SWP  ██

PID   USER   CPU%   MEM%   COMMAND
1234  bilal   80     5.2   python
2345  root    20     2.1   nginx

You can also use your arrow keys to select processes.

=> top vs htop

| `top`                                  | `htop`                     |
| -------------------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Usually already installed              | May need to install        |
| Basic interface                        | More user-friendly         |
| Keyboard based                         | Interactive                |
| Can manage processes                   | Easier to manage processes |
| Available on almost every Linux server | Not always installed       |

=> Install htop on Ubuntu:

sudo apt install htop

Then:

htop

